-- status  : Open
-- author  : @iris
-- created : 2026-10-31T09:21:06.666672+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/3fe4bf34-ea9f-4f2c-9e2d-63380991cba2
-- title:
--   Lemma 7: CLT for the recursion (33) (corrected: $\sup_k\mathbb E\|\zeta_k\|<\infty$)
-- statement:
--   Let $P\in\mathbb R^{n\times n}$ have spectral radius $\rho(P)<1$, let $G\in\mathbb R^{n\times m}$, $\rho\in(0,1)$, $N_k=\lceil\rho^{-(k+1)}\rceil$, $\alpha>0$, and let the noise $w_k=w_{k,N_k}$ satisfy (5) and Assumption 2 (ii) with limit covariance $S_0$. Let $e_k$ be generated by
--
--   $$
--   e_{k+1}=Pe_k-\alpha\rho^{-(k+1)/2}Gw_{k,N_k}+\zeta_{k+1},\tag{33}
--   $$
--
--   where $\mathbb E[\|e_0\|^2]<\infty$, the remainders $\zeta_k$ are measurable, $\zeta_k\to0$ in probability, and $\sup_{k}\mathbb E\|\zeta_k\|<\infty$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \alpha^{-1}e_k\ \xrightarrow{d}\ N(0,\Sigma)\qquad\text{with}\qquad \Sigma=\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}P^tGS_0G^T(P^t)^T ,
--   $$
--
--   the series being convergent.
--
--   This is the engine of all the CLTs of the paper: Theorems 1–4 write the rescaled error as (33) with a remainder that vanishes in probability.
--
--   **Correction.** As printed (without $\sup_k\mathbb E\|\zeta_k\|<\infty$) the lemma is false. Take $m=n=1$, $P=p\in(0,1)$, $G=1$, $\alpha=1$, $w\equiv0$ (so $S_0=0$), $e_0=0$ and independent events $A_t$ with $\mathbb P(A_t)=1/t$, and $\zeta_t=p^{-t}\mathbf 1_{A_t}$. Then $\zeta_t\to0$ in probability, but $e_k=\sum_{t\le k}p^{k-t}\zeta_t\ge1$ on $\bigcup_{\lceil k/2\rceil\le t\le k}A_t$, an event of probability at least $1/2$, so $e_k$ does not converge in distribution to $N(0,0)=\delta_0$. In Theorem 2 the remainder satisfies the added condition, because Proposition 2 gives $\mathbb E\|\zeta_k\|\to0$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $P$ is $n\times n$ and $G$ is $n\times m$ for arbitrary $n,m$ (Theorem 2 uses $n=2m$ and $G=\begin{pmatrix}I_m\\0_m\end{pmatrix}$). $\zeta_0$ does not enter (33), so the conditions on $\zeta$ are imposed for $k\ge1$. Convergence in probability is Mathlib's convergence in measure.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: ζ_k →P 0 → ζ_k →P 0, each ζ_k (k ≥ 1) measurable, and sup_k E‖ζ_k‖ < ∞; reason: false as printed: m = n = 1, P = p ∈ (0,1), G = 1, α = 1, w ≡ 0, e_0 = 0, ζ_t = p^{−t}1_{A_t} with independent P(A_t) = 1/t gives ζ_t →P 0 but P(e_k ≥ 1) ≥ 1/2, so e_k does not converge to N(0,0) = δ_0
--   - reading: {w_{k,N_k}} satisfies Assumption 2(ii) → w satisfies (5) for a filtration F (IsVRNoise) and (30)–(31) for the same F; reason: Assumption 2(ii) 'further' strengthens the standing (5), which the proof uses (p.41); without E[w_k | F_k] = 0 the lemma is false (deterministic w_k = N_k^{−1/2}v satisfies (30)–(31))
--   - reading: α (the step length) → α > 0; reason: α is the positive step length of Algorithms 1–3; α^{−1} requires α ≠ 0
--   - encoding: P, G square resp. compatible matrices → P : n × n, G : n × m for arbitrary n, m; reason: Theorem 2 uses n = 2m, G = (I_m; 0_m); same instances
--   - encoding: Σ ≜ Σ_t P^t G S0 Gᵀ (P^t)ᵀ → the conclusion also asserts summability of the series; reason: a non-summable tsum is 0 in Lean; the paper's definition presupposes convergence
-- source:
--   Lei, Shanbhag, Variance-Reduced Accelerated First-order Methods: Central Limit Theorems and Confidence Statements, arXiv:2006.07769v3, Lemma 7, eq. (33), p.16; proof in Appendix B, pp.40–42

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VRAccelCLT_specRad
import Definitions.Def_VRAccelCLT_batchSize
import Definitions.Def_VRAccelCLT_IsVRNoise
import Definitions.Def_VRAccelCLT_Assumption2ii
import Definitions.Def_VRAccelCLT_lyapSum

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter
open scoped Topology ENNReal NNReal

namespace VRAccelCLT

/-- **Lemma 7 (corrected): CLT for a linear recursion with geometric batch sizes.** Lei, Shanbhag,
arXiv:2006.07769v3, Lemma 7, p.16 (proof in Appendix B, pp.40–42): "Suppose that P is a square
matrix with spectral radius, denoted by ρ(P), strictly smaller than 1 (i.e., ρ(P) < 1),
Nk = ⌈ρ^{−(k+1)}⌉ with ρ ∈ (0, 1) for any k ≥ 0, and that {wk,Nk} satisfies Assumption 2(ii). Let
{ek} be generated by ek+1 = Pek − αρ^{−(k+1)/2}Gwk,Nk + ζk+1, (33) where E[∥e0∥2] < ∞ and
ζk →P 0. Then α^{−1}ek →d N(0, Σ) with Σ ≜ Σ_{t=0}^∞ P^t G S0 G^T (P^t)^T."

**Correction.** As printed the lemma is false: with `m = n = 1`, `P = p ∈ (0,1)`, `G = 1`, `α = 1`,
`w ≡ 0` (`S₀ = 0`), `e₀ = 0` and independent events `A_t` with `P(A_t) = 1/t`,
`ζ_t = p^{−t} 1_{A_t}` tends to `0` in probability, yet `e_k = Σ_{t≤k} p^{k−t} ζ_t ≥ 1` on
`⋃_{⌈k/2⌉≤t≤k} A_t`, an event of probability `≥ 1/2`, so `e_k` does not converge in distribution
to `N(0, 0) = δ₀`. The added hypothesis `sup_k E‖ζ_k‖ < ∞` (with each `ζ_k` measurable) repairs it;
the remainder in the proof of Theorem 2 satisfies it (Proposition 2 gives `E‖ζ_k‖ → 0`).

**Formalization Note.** `P` is `n × n` and `G` is `n × m` (Theorem 2 uses `n = 2m`, `G = (I; 0)`).
"`{w}` satisfies Assumption 2(ii)" is read with the standing (5) (`IsVRNoise`, for a filtration `ℱ`
and some `ν`), which Assumption 2(ii) "further" strengthens and the proof uses (p.41). `α > 0` is the
step length. `ζ₀` does not enter (33); the hypotheses on `ζ` concern `ζ_k`, `k ≥ 1`. The conclusion also asserts summability of the series defining `Σ` (a `tsum` of a
non-summable series is `0` in Lean). `→P` is `TendstoInMeasure`; `N(0, Σ)` is
`multivariateGaussian 0 Σ`. -/
theorem lemma_7 {n m : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace Ω} (μ : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0)
    (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (G : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin m) ℝ) (S0 : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ)
    (ρ α ν : ℝ) (w : ℕ → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m)) (e ζ : ℕ → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (hP : specRad P < 1) (hρ0 : 0 < ρ) (hρ1 : ρ < 1) (hα : 0 < α)
    (hw : IsVRNoise μ ℱ w ν (batchSize ρ)) (hw2 : Assumption2ii μ ℱ w (batchSize ρ) S0)
    (he0 : MemLp (e 0) 2 μ)
    (hζmeas : ∀ k, AEStronglyMeasurable (ζ (k + 1)) μ)
    (hζ : TendstoInMeasure μ ζ atTop (fun _ => 0))
    (hζbdd : ∃ C : ℝ≥0, ∀ k, ∫⁻ ω, (‖ζ (k + 1) ω‖₊ : ℝ≥0∞) ∂μ ≤ C)
    (hrec : ∀ (k : ℕ) (ω : Ω), e (k + 1) ω =
      Matrix.toEuclideanLin P (e k ω) -
        (α * ρ ^ (-((k : ℝ) + 1) / 2)) • Matrix.toEuclideanLin G (w k ω) + ζ (k + 1) ω) :
    Summable (fun t : ℕ => P ^ t * (G * S0 * G.transpose) * (P ^ t).transpose) ∧
    TendstoInDistribution (fun (k : ℕ) (ω : Ω) => α⁻¹ • e k ω) atTop id (fun _ => μ)
      (multivariateGaussian 0 (lyapSum P (G * S0 * G.transpose))) := by sorry

end VRAccelCLT
